---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const retrievalCode = `from google.adk.agents import Agent
from google.adk.tools import load_memory

def search_manuals(query: str, product_family: str | None = None) -> dict:
    """Search indexed manuals and troubleshooting notes."""
    matches = docs_index.search(
        query=query,
        product_family=product_family,
        top_k=5,
    )
    return {
        "results": [
            {
                "title": doc.title,
                "snippet": doc.snippet,
                "source": doc.source,
            }
            for doc in matches
        ]
    }

root_agent = Agent(
    model="gemini-2.5-flash",
    name="grounded_support_agent",
    instruction=(
        "Use retrieval before giving operational advice. "
        "Search manuals for current procedures and load memory when the user "
        "asks about prior cases."
    ),
    tools=[search_manuals, load_memory],
)`;
---

<SiteLayout current="search-retrieval" title="Search and retrieval">
  <section class="section page-section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Tool form 03E</p>
      <h1>Search and retrieval ground the agent in fresh evidence.</h1>
      <p>
        Memory is where information lives. Search and retrieval are how the
        agent goes to get it. That makes them a tool form: the model chooses
        when to fetch external or stored evidence instead of guessing from
        weights alone.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="signal-panel signal-panel--definitions" style="margin-top: 32px; margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div>
        <article>
          <span>Grounding</span>
          <strong>Search public or changing sources.</strong>
          <p>Use web search, docs lookup, or database queries when the answer depends on current facts, external references, or information that was never inside the model to begin with.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Retrieval</span>
          <strong>Pull from private knowledge stores.</strong>
          <p>Retrieval is the act of fetching the most relevant internal documents, tickets, manuals, or chunks from an indexed corpus so the next step is tied to company-specific evidence.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Recall</span>
          <strong>Search memory explicitly.</strong>
          <p>Past sessions do not magically stay in context. In ADK-style systems, memory becomes useful only when the agent calls a retrieval interface such as <code>load_memory</code>.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-head compact-head" style="margin-top: 40px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">The loop</p>
      <h2>Grounding is a retrieval workflow, not a single magic step.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="mini-diagram react-diagram" aria-label="Retrieval flow diagram" style="margin-bottom: 32px;">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 160" role="img">
        <defs>
          <marker id="react-arrow" markerHeight="8" markerWidth="8" orient="auto" refX="7" refY="4">
            <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>
        <g class="react-links">
          <path d="M 124 56 H 180"></path>
          <path d="M 300 56 H 356"></path>
          <path d="M 476 56 H 532"></path>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg">
          <rect x="24" y="34" width="100" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="74" y="60">Query</text>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg is-planner">
          <rect x="180" y="34" width="120" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="240" y="60">Fetch</text>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg">
          <rect x="356" y="34" width="120" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="416" y="60">Inject</text>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg is-done">
          <rect x="532" y="34" width="100" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="582" y="60">Answer</text>
        </g>
        <text class="loop-caption" x="320" y="116">turn request into query, fetch evidence, then act</text>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <ul class="timeline-list">
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">01</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Form the query</strong>
          <p>The agent turns the user request into something searchable: keywords, filters, entity names, time bounds, or a more retrieval-friendly rewrite.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">02</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Fetch candidates</strong>
          <p>The runtime queries a search engine, vector index, document store, or memory service and returns a bounded set of relevant results instead of the whole archive.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">03</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Inject evidence</strong>
          <p>The retrieved snippets become context for the next model step. Good systems keep this payload compact, because grounding that overflows the window stops being grounding.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">04</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Answer or act</strong>
          <p>The model now reasons over evidence that is current, private, or previously stored. If confidence is still low, it can search again instead of pretending the first fetch was enough.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mechanism-note">
      Search is the broad family: web, docs, SQL, vector, graph. Retrieval is
      the narrower mechanic: fetch the most relevant evidence for this turn.
      Memory is neither of those by itself; it is a store that becomes useful
      only when the agent retrieves from it.
    </p>

    <figure class="code-card code-card--standalone">
      <figcaption>
        <span class="lang-pill">python</span>
        <span class="code-filename">google_adk_search_retrieval.py</span>
        <a href="https://adk.dev/sessions/memory/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google ADK memory docs</a>
      </figcaption>
      <Code code={retrievalCode} lang="python" theme="github-light" />
    </figure>

    <div class="section-head compact-head" style="margin-top: 48px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Further reading</p>
      <h2>Grounding and retrieval references.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="refs-grid">
      <a class="refs-block" href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/google-search" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        <span>Web grounding</span>
        <h3>Google Search grounding</h3>
        <p>
          A concrete example of search as a tool: the model asks for fresh
          public information and receives grounded results instead of relying on
          static pretraining alone.
        </p>
        <div class="refs-link">ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/google-search <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="refs-block" href="https://adk.dev/sessions/memory/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        <span>Memory recall</span>
        <h3>Google ADK Memory</h3>
        <p>
          The important ADK idea for this page: memory is explicit. Sessions can
          be ingested into a memory service, then searched later through a tool
          such as <code>load_memory</code>.
        </p>
        <div class="refs-link">adk.dev/sessions/memory <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="refs-block" href="https://cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/enterprise-search-introduction" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        <span>Enterprise retrieval</span>
        <h3>Vertex AI Search</h3>
        <p>
          A practical reference for retrieval over private corpora: indexing
          internal documents, ranking relevant passages, and grounding answers
          on organization-specific knowledge.
        </p>
        <div class="refs-link">cloud.google.com/.../enterprise-search-introduction <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
